# What is translated of this program's messages, in Indonesian.
#
# Keyed by the English wording, the same as the German file beside it.
# All of the program's texts stand here -- the captions the window
# carries and the report that runs beside them, Resolve included. Every
# text that is not in this file appears in English: T() gives back the
# key it was handed when it finds no entry, so what is missing reads
# rather than breaks.
#
# These translations were written by Claude, an Anthropic model, out of
# what it knows. No translation service was asked, no Indonesian speaker
# has read them, and nobody has reviewed a single line. Treat every
# entry as a first draft. Correcting one costs nothing but this file:
# delete an entry and that text is English again.
#
# They were written on 24.9.2026 in two steps: a glossary first, which
# fixed the form of address and one word for each thing, and then every
# text against it, three languages at a time from one sheet. Every
# entry was read back once in full by another model, against the
# English and those words, and 33 were put right; a third
# refuted what was not a fault.
#
# Anyone who speaks Indonesian is welcome to improve this file, and one
# entry at a time is enough -- nothing else has to be understood. Find
# the entry by its English msgid, change only the msgstr under it, and
# leave the msgid exactly as it stands: it is the key the program looks
# the text up by, and a changed msgid loses the entry rather than
# correcting it.
#
# One entry per message: msgid is the English wording the
# program is written in, msgstr what is said instead. An
# entry left out appears in English.
#
# A counted thing is the exception to that one entry. It carries
# msgid_plural and one msgstr[i] per wording this language has, in
# place of the single msgstr, and the Plural-Forms line in the
# header below says how many that is and which count wants which.
# One here: the wording does not change with the count at
# all, and msgstr[0] is the whole of it -- for one, for two
# and for nought alike. Indonesian leaves a noun as it is
# after a number, 1 kamera and 3 kamera, so where the English
# has two wordings this file has one; that is Indonesian,
# not a form left out.
#
# The wordings are read as a list, so a form left empty costs that
# form and not the entry. Every form carries the same %s the
# English wording does: the program applies the count to whatever
# comes back, and a form without it stops the run on a number
# nobody tried.
#
# Neither English wording has an ordinary entry of its own beside
# such a group: the singular would be the same msgid twice, and the
# plural is never asked for on its own -- the program asks the group
# by its singular at every count. Where the rule cannot be read at
# all, a counted thing comes out English.
